  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Answer Key That Talks Back",
    "overview": "A learning site held to the category standard of Brilliant, Khan Academy, Duolingo web and BBC Learning English. The page is a white sheet with ink text; nothing decorates it. Each idea is stated in plain prose and then immediately handed to the student as something to touch: a verb to switch, a word to drop in a gap, a chip to sort. Every check answers with an explanation, never a bare right or wrong.\n\nDensity is calm and reading-first: a 68ch prose column, generous section rhythm, large tappable answers sized for a thumb on a phone. Color is spent on meaning only. One blue means \"act here\", green and red mean \"you were right / you were wrong\", and six small pattern hues carry the same code as the printed learning guide so the screen and the paper agree. Motion is quick, eased out and purposeful: words morph, wrong answers shake, feedback slides open.\n\nThe system rejects the decorated course portal: banner heroes, stock photography, icon-card walls, drop-shadowed tiles.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White ground, ink text, hairline 1px borders, 12px corners, flat surfaces.",
      "One action blue for primaries, selection and progress; nothing else is blue.",
      "Green and red appear only as answer feedback.",
      "Six pattern hues as small markers (badges, highlighted verb phrases, dots), always with their soft tint.",
      "Geist throughout, semibold and tightly tracked at display sizes.",
      "Every practice block lives in the same bordered Activity frame with Check, Feedback, Next, Score, Try again."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Blue Rule",
        "body": "Action blue marks the thing to press or the thing chosen, and nothing else. Never use it as decoration, section tint or illustration fill.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Feedback-Only Rule",
        "body": "Green and red appear only after the student checks an answer (plus the done tick and the perfect score). Never use them for headings, callouts, warnings or brand accents.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Guide Code Rule",
        "body": "Pattern hue N always means pattern N, matching the printed learning guide. A new lesson that has no numbered patterns does not borrow these hues for decoration; a new lesson with its own categories maps them in order and keeps them small.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Plain Voice Rule",
        "body": "Student-facing text avoids em dashes and middle dots. Use commas, colons, periods or a pipe (\"LI436 | 2026-2\") for separators.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Count Rule",
        "body": "Scores, counts, weeks and badge numerals use tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Card Rule",
        "body": "Cards, Activity frames, callouts and panels have no shadow. Depth comes from a 1px line border or a surface fill.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lift-On-Drag Rule",
        "body": "A shadow means \"you are holding this\". It appears on a chip in the drag state and nowhere else.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** wrap every new practice block in the Activity frame and end it with Feedback that explains the answer, then Score and Try again.",
      "**Do** build new lessons in MDX from the registered blocks (Section, PatternMorph, ChoiceSet, SortBuckets, InlineChoice, GapFill, SpotMistake, SentenceBuilder, StopTimeline, FlipCards, Contrast, Examples, Tip, VerbFinder, VerbList, Results) before inventing a new component.",
      "**Do** keep corners at 12px for containers and answers, 8px for chips, 6px for in-line marks.",
      "**Do** keep tappable answers at 44px minimum height (48px for answer options) and text inputs at 16px to avoid phone zoom.",
      "**Do** mark the grammar part of an example with the pattern hue on its soft tint, and use the same hue for that pattern everywhere on the page.",
      "**Do** honor prefers-reduced-motion: every morph and slide must collapse to a fade or nothing.",
      "**Do** write student-facing copy without em dashes or middle dots."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** put shadows on cards, Activity frames, callouts or buttons; shadow is reserved for a dragged chip.",
      "**Don't** introduce a second action color or use blue decoratively.",
      "**Don't** use green or red outside answer feedback (no green \"tip\" or red \"careful\" callouts; callouts stay neutral surface with ink icons).",
      "**Don't** reassign pattern hues to new meanings or enlarge them into backgrounds and banners.",
      "**Don't** add banner heroes, stock photography or icon-card grids.",
      "**Don't** add small label lines (eyebrows or kickers) above headings; the heading and its metadata row carry the context.",
      "**Don't** add a new type family or set display text in a system face; Geist through next/font only."
    ]
  }
}